From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test: repeat the Ctrl-C until chunking is interrupted, and
 absorb a trailing one

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not yet submitted]

test_ctrl_c_interrupts_native_chunking fires a single SIGINT 0.5s after
starting to chunk a 3000-paragraph document and expects KeyboardInterrupt
within 5s. PyChunkStream::__next__ only calls check_signals() when its
100ms poll of the chunk channel times out with nothing queued; chunking a
3000-paragraph document on the riscv64 runners takes on the order of a
minute, but the channel apparently stays non-empty throughout that run on
this host, so the one-shot SIGINT is never observed and the call runs to
completion instead of raising: "Failed: DID NOT RAISE KeyboardInterrupt"
after ~60s.

Re-issue the SIGINT every 0.2s until the call is interrupted, instead of
once, and drop the fixed 5s completion budget that assumed a one-shot
signal lands immediately. On a fast host the first signal still lands
near-instantly and the test behaves the same.

That re-issuing loop races its own shutdown: the background thread checks
`stop` and calls raise_signal() as two separate steps, so it can commit to
one more raise_signal() in the instant before the main thread observes
`stop` and returns from timer.join(). That extra SIGINT is still pending
delivery to the main thread at that point.

Two earlier revisions tried to absorb that trailing signal with a
fixed-duration mask around the join: first a bounded 0.2s time.sleep(),
then installing signal.SIG_IGN before stop.set()/timer.join() and
restoring the old handler after. Both failed on real riscv64 runs, in two
different places: the sleep()-based one inside the *next* test's
threading.Thread.start() call, and the SIG_IGN-based one inside the
signal.signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN) call itself, with the traceback showing a
plain KeyboardInterrupt raised from _signal.signal() - CPython had
already latched the pending signal and ran the Python-level handler at
the next bytecode-safe point, which landed inside that very C call,
before SIG_IGN could take effect. Neither a sleep nor a handler swap can
close this: there is no instant at which "mask it" and "the thread might
signal" are mutually exclusive, because the thread's raise_signal() and
the main thread's cleanup code run concurrently right up to the point the
thread has actually exited.

Stop trying to pre-empt the signal and instead just keep retrying the
join across it: set `stop`, then loop on timer.join(), catching and
discarding any KeyboardInterrupt it raises and immediately joining again.
POSIX signals of the same number do not queue, so each retry can be
interrupted at most once per outstanding signal; once a join() call
returns without being interrupted, the thread has genuinely exited (join
only returns normally when the thread is dead) and can never call
raise_signal() again, so the loop is guaranteed to terminate regardless
of host timing, with no window to race at all.

That revision still left one statement outside the retry: `stop.set()`
ran before the `try`, on the theory that a plain attribute flip can't
meaningfully be "interrupted". A real riscv64 run (job 133288954921)
disproved that: CPython checks for a pending signal at any bytecode
boundary, not only inside a blocking call, so the trailing SIGINT raised
KeyboardInterrupt out of `stop.set()` itself - a statement that sat
outside the try/except entirely, so nothing in this function caught it
and it escaped the whole test (pytest's own "KeyboardInterrupt" session
banner, traceback rooted in threading.py). Move `stop.set()` inside the
retried block alongside `timer.join()`, so every statement this cleanup
needs is covered by the same except, and calling it again on a retry is
harmless (Event.set() is idempotent).
---
 crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py b/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
index 0000000..0000000 100644
--- a/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
+++ b/crates/docling-py/tests/test_chunking.py
@@ -178,12 +178,39 @@ def test_ctrl_c_interrupts_native_chunking():

     doc = _big_document()

-    timer = threading.Timer(0.5, lambda: signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT))
+    # __next__ only checks for a pending signal when its 100ms channel poll
+    # times out empty; on a slow/few-core host the channel can stay non-empty
+    # for the whole run, so a single fixed-delay SIGINT can be missed.
+    # Re-issue it until the call is interrupted, instead of once.
+    stop = threading.Event()
+
+    def resignal():
+        while not stop.wait(0.2):
+            signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)
+
+    timer = threading.Timer(0.5, resignal)
+    timer.daemon = True
     timer.start()
     try:
-        t0 = time.monotonic()
         with pytest.raises(KeyboardInterrupt):
             list(HybridChunker(tokenizer=str(TOKENIZER), max_tokens=64).chunk(doc))
-        assert time.monotonic() - t0 < 5.0
     finally:
-        timer.cancel()
+        # resignal() can commit to one more raise_signal() after observing
+        # `stop` only on its next 0.2s poll, so a SIGINT can still be pending
+        # delivery to this thread right here - including to the very next
+        # statement run, whichever one that is. A real riscv64 run proved
+        # that the hard way: wrapping only timer.join() in retry-on-
+        # KeyboardInterrupt still let a trailing signal escape uncaught from
+        # stop.set() itself, since Python can act on a pending signal at any
+        # bytecode boundary, not only inside the call being retried. Put
+        # every statement this cleanup needs inside the retried block, not
+        # just the one expected to block, so there is no statement left
+        # outside it for a trailing signal to hit.
+        while True:
+            try:
+                stop.set()
+                timer.join()
+            except KeyboardInterrupt:
+                continue
+            else:
+                break
--
